                <pb facs="0368" n="269" sameAs="frus:frus1936v05/pg269"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">632.6231/69a: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of
                            State to the Ambassador in Brazil</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Gibson</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1936-06-09T19:00:00-04:00">June 9, 1936—7 p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>87. A United Press report and a special cable to the <hi rendition="#italic">New
                        York Times</hi>, both dated Bio, June 8, report that the Foreign Minister
                    and the German Chargé d’Affaires exchanged notes on that date “sealing the
                    one-year trade agreement between the Reichsbank and the Banco do Brasil” on a
                    basis similar to that reported in your 137 [<hi rendition="#italic">139</hi>],
                    May 30, 3 p.m. The quotas mentioned in these press despatches are identical with
                    those mentioned in your 137 [<hi rendition="#italic">139</hi>], but the sales of
                    German goods by categories are to be held to 1935 limits. The Department is
                    receiving inquiries on the basis of these despatches and would appreciate your
                    prompt comment.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Phillips</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
